logs/

# Local-only property-based soak harness (run.ts header: "NOT a repo artifact —
# never committed"). Source + 9MB run logs/ledgers stay on the operator's machine.
scripts/overnight-soak/

# BMAD (bmad-method) — the framework itself is committed so the whole team
# gets the same agents, but two classes of file are per-developer:
#   config.user.toml holds install answers scoped to one person (the agent's
#   user_name comes from whoever ran the installer), and it is regenerated on
#   every install — committing it would hand your name to everyone else.
#   _bmad-output/ is generated planning and implementation artifacts.
_bmad/config.user.toml
_bmad/custom/config.user.toml
_bmad-output/
# Installer-generated integrity manifest: 265 rows of "path,sha256" that the
# installer rewrites on every install/update. Excluded for two reasons. It is
# churn — every BMAD update rewrites all 265 hashes. And it trips the gitleaks
# generic-api-key rule: the row for bmad-ux/assets/key-screens.md puts the
# literal "key" next to a 64-char hex digest, which reads as a credential. It
# is a content hash, not a secret, but the honest fix is to stop committing a
# generated file rather than to add a path exemption to the secret scanner —
# see this repo's own note in .gitleaks.toml about what a broad exemption cost
# last time. Verified `bmad status` behaves identically without it.
_bmad/_config/files-manifest.csv
